# Outputs And Effects

An Action returns a result. It can also return a list of effect requests. An
*effect request* is plain data that asks the caller to do something after the
work succeeds, such as send an email or write an audit entry:

```text
{:ok, map_or_output}
{:ok, map_or_output, requests}
```

`Jido.Exec` returns the requests with the result. It never performs them. Your
application decides how and when to act on them.

This Livebook approves an order and prepares a CSV export. It runs each
example as an Action, a Flow, an Instruction, and a managed execution. The
[Effect Rules](#effect-rules) section at the end lists the complete contract.
No email or audit service is needed.

## Set Up

This Livebook installs `jido_action` from a local checkout. Before you start
Livebook, set `JIDO_ACTION_PATH` to the absolute path of your `jido_action`
folder. The path must be available to the Livebook runtime. Run the cells in
order.

```elixir
package_path = System.fetch_env!("JIDO_ACTION_PATH")

Mix.install([{:jido_action, path: package_path}], lockfile: Path.join(package_path, "mix.lock"))
```

## Return A Map With An Optional Request

The Action creates an approval result. If `notify` is true, it also requests
an order confirmation. The caller owns the state update and notification.

```elixir
defmodule EffectExamples.ApproveOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "example_approve_order",
    description: "Prepares an order approval and an optional confirmation request",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        order_id: Zoi.string(),
        notify: Zoi.boolean() |> Zoi.default(false)
      }),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string(), status: Zoi.enum([:approved])})

  @impl true
  def run(%{order_id: order_id, notify: notify}, _context) do
    approval = %{order_id: order_id, status: :approved}

    if notify do
      {:ok, approval, [{:send_confirmation, order_id}]}
    else
      {:ok, approval}
    end
  end
end
```

Run the same Action directly and inside a Flow. The Flow returns the same map
and requests without changes to the Action.

```elixir
alias Jido.Action.Output
alias Jido.{Exec, Flow, Instruction}
alias Jido.Flow.Ref

approval_flow =
  Flow.new!(%{
    name: "approve_order",
    components: [
      %{
        kind: :step,
        name: "approve",
        action: EffectExamples.ApproveOrder,
        params: Ref.input([])
      }
    ],
    output: Ref.result("approve")
  })

for target <- [EffectExamples.ApproveOrder, approval_flow] do
  {:ok, %{order_id: "order-42", status: :approved}} =
    Exec.run(target, %{order_id: "order-42"})

  {:ok, %{order_id: "order-42", status: :approved}, [{:send_confirmation, "order-42"}]} =
    Exec.run(target, %{order_id: "order-42", notify: true})
end
```

Omit the third element when there are no requests. Returning `[]` gives the
same two-element result.

## Return A Stream With An Optional Request

A normal success result is a map. `Jido.Action.Output` is an envelope for a
success value that is intentionally not a map. Build it with `Output.raw/2`,
`Output.stream/2`, `Output.batch/2`, or `Output.opaque/2`. The optional
`meta:` map describes the value.

The export Action reads its rows from context. Each row has integer `order_id`
and `total_cents` fields. The Action builds a lazy CSV stream and can request
an audit entry that records when the export was prepared.

```elixir
defmodule EffectExamples.ExportOrders do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "example_export_orders",
    description: "Prepares a lazy CSV stream and an optional audit request",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        report_id: Zoi.string(),
        audit: Zoi.boolean() |> Zoi.default(false)
      })

  @impl true
  def run(%{report_id: report_id, audit: audit}, context) do
    case Map.fetch(context, :rows) do
      {:ok, rows} ->
        lines = Stream.map(rows, fn row -> "#{row.order_id},#{row.total_cents}\n" end)
        stream = Stream.concat(["order_id,total_cents\n"], lines)

        output =
          Jido.Action.Output.stream(stream,
            meta: %{content_type: "text/csv", report_id: report_id}
          )

        if audit do
          requests = [{:audit, %{event: :export_prepared, report_id: report_id}}]
          {:ok, output, requests}
        else
          {:ok, output}
        end

      :error ->
        {:error, :missing_sales_rows}
    end
  end
end
```

`Output.meta` describes the stream. The third tuple element holds only effect
requests. Exec does not read the rows or perform the audit request.

```elixir
export_flow =
  Flow.new!(%{
    name: "export_orders",
    components: [
      %{
        kind: :step,
        name: "export",
        action: EffectExamples.ExportOrders,
        params: Ref.input([])
      }
    ],
    output: Ref.result("export")
  })

rows = [%{order_id: 42, total_cents: 1250}, %{order_id: 43, total_cents: 750}]
context = %{rows: rows}
params = %{report_id: "report-7", audit: true}
expected_csv = "order_id,total_cents\n42,1250\n43,750\n"

for target <- [EffectExamples.ExportOrders, export_flow] do
  {:ok, %Output{kind: :stream, value: stream},
   [{:audit, %{event: :export_prepared, report_id: "report-7"}}]} =
    Exec.run(target, params, context)

  # The caller consumes the stream after Exec has returned.
  ^expected_csv = Enum.join(stream)

  {:ok, %Output{kind: :stream}} =
    Exec.run(target, %{report_id: "report-7"}, context)
end
```

The audit event means **export prepared**. It does not mean that all rows were
read or sent. A row source can fail later, while the caller consumes the
stream. That failure cannot take back a request that Exec has already
returned. If you need an audit entry for completed delivery, create it after
you consume and deliver the stream.

Keep the row source available while you consume the stream. A resource that
the Action owns can close when the Action returns.

## Use Instructions

An Instruction gives Exec the target, parameters, and context as one value.

```elixir
instruction = Instruction.new!(target: export_flow, params: params, context: context)
expected_requests = [{:audit, %{event: :export_prepared, report_id: "report-7"}}]

{:ok, %Output{value: stream}, ^expected_requests} = Exec.run(instruction)
^expected_csv = Enum.join(stream)
```

## Handle Failure

Without a row source, the export fails before it creates the stream. Exec
returns an error and no audit request.

```elixir
{:error, _error} = Exec.run(export_flow, params)
```

In a Flow with several Actions, only a successful run returns requests. If any
component fails or times out, Exec returns `{:error, error}` and drops the
requests of components that already succeeded.

## See The Request Order

A Flow returns one list with the requests of every component. This Flow has
three independent components and one component that waits for `"zeta"`:

```elixir
defmodule EffectExamples.Request do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "example_request",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{tag: Zoi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{tag: tag}, _context), do: {:ok, %{tag: tag}, [{:request, tag}]}
end

order_flow =
  Flow.new!(%{
    name: "request_order",
    components: [
      %{kind: :step, name: "zeta", action: EffectExamples.Request, params: %{tag: "zeta"}},
      %{
        kind: :step,
        name: "after_zeta",
        action: EffectExamples.Request,
        params: %{tag: "after_zeta"},
        needs: ["zeta"]
      },
      %{kind: :step, name: "alpha", action: EffectExamples.Request, params: %{tag: "alpha"}},
      %{
        kind: :map,
        name: "items",
        collection: Ref.input(:items),
        action: EffectExamples.Request,
        params: %{tag: Ref.item()}
      }
    ],
    output: %{done: true}
  })

{:ok, %{done: true}, requests} =
  Exec.run(order_flow, %{items: ["i1", "i2"]}, %{}, max_concurrency: 4)

[
  {:request, "alpha"},
  {:request, "i1"},
  {:request, "i2"},
  {:request, "zeta"},
  {:request, "after_zeta"}
] = requests
```

`"alpha"`, `"items"`, and `"zeta"` have no dependencies, so they appear in
name order. `"after_zeta"` depends on `"zeta"`, so it comes after it. The Map
component keeps item order. The list is the same for every `max_concurrency`
value.

## Read Requests From A Managed Execution

`Jido.Exec.start/6` runs a target under a supervised `Runic.Runner`. See
[Managed Execution](managed-execution.md). There, the requests are stored in
the result's metadata at `meta.jido.effects`:

```elixir
{:ok, _runner} = Runic.Runner.start_link(name: EffectExamples.Runner)
parent = self()

{:ok, _worker} =
  Exec.start(
    EffectExamples.Runner,
    "approve-order-42",
    approval_flow,
    %{order_id: "order-42", notify: true},
    %{},
    on_complete: fn id, _workflow -> send(parent, {:execution_complete, id}) end
  )

receive do
  {:execution_complete, "approve-order-42"} -> :ok
after
  5_000 -> raise "the managed execution did not complete"
end

{:ok, %{result: fact}} =
  Runic.Runner.get_results(EffectExamples.Runner, "approve-order-42", facts: true)

%{order_id: "order-42", status: :approved} = fact.value
[{:send_confirmation, "order-42"}] = fact.meta.jido.effects

dedup_key =
  Exec.effect_id("approve-order-42", fact.causal_ancestry.activation_id, fact.id, 0)
```

A retry or a resume can repeat work. `Jido.Exec.effect_id/4` derives the same
key for the same request every time. Store that key with the delivery record
so that you perform each request once.

## Effect Rules

- **Shape.** The third success element is a proper list. Any other value
  fails with an `ExecutionFailureError` whose `details.reason` is
  `:invalid_effects`. An empty list gives `{:ok, result}`.
- **Errors.** Exec discards the third element of `{:error, reason, effects}`.
- **Flow failure.** A failed, timed-out, or cancelled Flow returns
  `{:error, error}` with no requests, even from components that succeeded.
- **Order.** A Flow lists requests in dependency order, then component name.
  A component keeps the order of its own list. A nested Flow's requests take
  its position in the parent. Map, Reduce, and Iterate requests follow item
  or iteration order. A Dispatch lists decision requests, then expander
  requests, then the requests of the target it continues to.
- **No delivery.** Exec never performs a request and never consumes a stream
  output. Your application owns delivery and its retry policy.
- **Managed execution.** The requests are in `fact.meta.jido.effects` of the
  result fact. Use `Jido.Exec.effect_id/4` for deduplication keys.

## Run The Example Tests

From the `jido_action` folder:

```shell
mix test test/examples/action_effects_test.exs
```

The tests run this Livebook's Elixir cells against the current package
source. They also check lazy consumption, optional effects, agreement between
entry points, and failure before and after stream creation.
